<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Spanish Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1778 CE – 1968 CE · capital Santa Isabel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spanish colonial territories in the Gulf of Guinea from 1778 to 1968, built on cacao plantations on Fernando Po, which became independent as Equatorial Guinea.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> acquired the territory in 1778 by the Treaty of El Pardo with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> and until 1810 administered it through the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept a base on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Bioko</a> from 1827 to 1843, and Spanish rule was restored in 1844. Captain Carlos Chacón arrived in 1858 as the first governor. By the treaty of Paris in 1900 Spain was left with the continental enclave of Río Muni.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Planters developed large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> plantations on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Fernando Po</a>, where the indigenous Bubi population had been decimated by disease and forced labour, so the economy depended on imported contract workers from Liberia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. In 1930 an International Labour Organization commission found that Liberian workers had been recruited under conditions of criminal compulsion scarcely distinguishable from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> fought campaigns against the Fang in the 1920s, and the whole colony was considered pacified by 1929. Between 1926 and 1959 Bioko and Río Muni were united as the colony of Spanish Guinea. By 1968 almost 100,000 ethnic Nigerians were living and working there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1956 the colony became the Province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>, and in 1959 a law declared Guineans equal in rights to Spaniards. A Regime of Autonomy, approved by referendum in 1963, gave formal self-government that was in some sense illusory. Francisco Macías Nguema won the presidential election in September 1968, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> handed power to the new government of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/equatorial-guinea">Equatorial Guinea</a> on 12 October 1968.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/spanish-guinea.jpg" alt="Spanish Guinea" width="330" height="388"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Portuguese_and_Spanish_Guinea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1778 CE – 1968 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santa Isabel</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carlos Chacón, Francisco Macías Nguema</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Spanish Catholic</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Pidgin English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Equatorial Guinea</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guinea Española</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1873&amp;focus=spanish-guinea" aria-label="Spanish Guinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4967 2118 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Spanish Guinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5287 2374L5278 2377"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5278 2377h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5287 2374h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5287 2374h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1873&amp;focus=spanish-guinea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
